  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA MCS pushed out of Lake Erie this morning and is currently\nimpacting WNY and WPA. The system has spilt its motion, with \nthe northern part spinning up a MCV and moving to the ENE while \nthe southern portion of the MCS has continued to progress to the\nSE, with new storms forming along the outflow across SE Ohio. \nCurrently, the main concern is the MCV traveling eastward toward\nCNY. CIN remains over the western portion of the area and has \nalready dissipated a cluster of storms moving onto Steuben \ncounty. As the day progresses, the CIN is expected to break and \nMLCAPE values in the 1200-1700 j/kg range are expected to \ndevelop over portions of CNY and NEPA. The difference today from\nyesterday's forecast is that 0-6km bulk shear has increased, \nnow with 30-40kts of effective bulk shear expected to be present\nover the area as the MCV moves in with increasing low level \ninstability. These ingredients lead to a chance for organized \nclusters of storms to develop, with damaging winds, hail and \neven an isolated tornado possible. Best chances for this \ncontinue to be across the Southern Tier into NEPA where we are \nexpected to see the best CAPE/shear profiles, but an isolated \nsevere storm is not out of the question to the north of this \narea. Heavy rain will accompany some of these storms, and with \nthe recent heavy rains, isolated flash flooding across small \nstreams and urban areas is possible. Storms from the MCV should \nbe east of the area around 8pm. Some guidance wants to bring in \nanother round of showers and storms across the Mohawk valley \nlater this evening into the early overnight hours as some \nstronger shear and instability work their way in from Lake \nOntario.\n\nPrior to the showers and storms moving through, temperatures will \nrise into the upper 70s to mid 80s. Humidity will increase as\nthe day progresses, with dew points currently in the low to mid\n60s rising to the upper 60s to low 70s. Overnight, temperatures\nwill be fairly mild, only falling into the 60s. With calming \nwinds, breaks in cloud cover, and added moisture from today's \nshowers, patchy fog will be possible in the valleys.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\n***Much of the extended forecast remains on track from the\n previous discussion. Uncertainty remains with timing and track\n of the passing shortwaves.*** \n\nA ridge out west and broad troughing in the east will favor a fairly \nactive pattern this week. Ridge riders will pass over the Great \nLakes Region and pass near or over our area. Weak high pressure\nand drier air will build in the night prior, though a weak \nsystem will likely clip the Southern Tier and northeast PA on \nTuesday with passing showers and thunderstorms. Wednesday is \nexpected to be mostly dry as well\n\nLate in the week, the upper level trough begins to dig slightly and \nleads to the next system moving in Wednesday night into Thursday. \nHigh pressure then follows to bring another dry period late in the \nweek and to start the weekend. A weak low pressure then moves \nthrough Sunday with another batch of showers that may linger into \nthe start of next week. \n\nThere is quite bit of uncertainty with each system mentioned above, \nespecially late in the week and over the weekend. Models continue to \ndiffer on exact tracks and timing, which impacts the coverage. \nThere is confidence that there will be dry periods and not any\nparticular day is expected to be a wash out.\n\nTemperatures will trend cooler during the later half of the week. \nHighs will be in the 70s to low 80s. Temperatures overnight will \nfall into the 50s.",
